    "lawCode": "Amd §461-l, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5840\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the social services law, in relation\nto establishing a certificate of need process for the assisted living\nprogram; and to establish a moratorium on the authorization of new\nassisted living programs in certain cases\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo create a certificate of need process for the assisted living\nprogram.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill deletes provisions requiring a competitive\nsolicitation process for assisted living program (ALP) beds and\ncreates a new certificate of need (CON) process that awards ALP beds\nbased on demonstrated community need. In order to reduce the state\ncost of reviewing duplicative filings, the bill also allows existing\nALP providers to add up to nine beds through an expedited review\nprocess. Finally, the bill also allows the director of budget to place\na moratorium on the issuance of new beds if he or she determines that\nadditional ALP beds would result in a net negative fiscal impact to\nthe Medicaid program.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides an effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nFirst created in the early 1990s, ALP has proven to be a highly\nsuccessful lower-cost alternative to nursing home care. ALP has\nexpanded from its original allocation of 4,000 beds to over 12,000\nbeds, all of which have been awarded through an episodic competitive\nsolicitation process. A consequence of awarding beds through this\nsolicitation process has been that 18 upstate counties have no ALP\nbeds and another 18 counties have fewer than 50 ALP beds. Many small\nrural providers do not have the resources and expertise to effectively\ncompete in a statewide solicitation. The solicitations have also been\nunpredictable and inconsistent.\n\nConverting the ALP from a competitive solicitation process to a CON\nprocess would alleviate disparities within the ALP and bring the ALP\ninto the same regulatory framework as every other type of health care\nfacility. The CON process will allow expansion of ALP beds to counties\nthat have a demonstrated need in a rational and timely manner.\nFurther, the department of health will be relieved of the\nadministratively burdensome competitive bid process, and, instead,\nwill be able to use the CON process to determine county long-term care\nrequirements holistically. The department's use of the ALP in the\ncontinuum of long-term care CON applications may result in savings, as\nindependent residents who are nursing home eligible can appropriately\nbe placed in the lower cost ALP alternative. This alternative is\ncritical in a managed care environment and is consistent with MRT\ngoals.\n\nAs the state confronts an aging population, ALP beds are a critical\ncomponent of long-term care providers, as they allow residents to \"age\nin place\" and reduce overall Medicaid costs by providing lower cost\nalternatives to nursing homes. However, if for any reason, savings do\nnot result from additional ALP beds, this legislation allows the\ndirector of budget to place a moratorium on ALP beds when he or she\ndetermines there is a net negative fiscal impact on Medicaid.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nOverall, fiscal savings will accrue due to reduced institutional\nplacements.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
